I'm submitting **r10-ldef-real-stel-disp**. It is a small, real gain: on the rented B200 it is S −0.8%, M −1.2%, L +0.3% against r6. The emulator predicts 0.611 ± 0.009 on the portal against 0.610 for r6, a 55% chance of beating it. The exploration idea itself, engineering which outputs are left in L2 at the end, did not pan out: no store-side variant moved any band by 3%.

- **Store side is already at its limit.** Under harness timing, evict_last on every store beats plain stores by 8–19% at 128–2048 tokens and 3–5% at 4096–8192, so r5's gain reproduces. Nothing beyond that helped by more than ±1% at 1024–8192 tokens: evict_last on only the last 48/80/112 MB, early stores evict_normal or evict_first, fractional policies 0.5/0.75, or a different block order.
- **The hidden cost is on the read side.** The harness's zero-fill flush leaves dirty lines in L2. A read-only kernel takes 7–11 µs longer after it than after a clean start, a copy 5–6 µs longer, and a write-only kernel is unaffected. Marking the loads `L2::evict_first` makes a read-only kernel 15% faster at 100 MB and 13% at 400 MB.
- **Most of that read-side gain doesn't survive evict_last stores.** In the full kernel, evict_first loads give −8% at 586 tokens and −1 to −2% at 256–1024 tokens. They are +1.5% at 2048 and neutral at L. r6 already took the 586-token gain, so the only new part is applying evict_first loads up to 1024 tokens instead of 600.
- **I had r6's loads wrong at first.** An early probe made it look as if r6's evict_first policy constant did nothing. A later probe, with the constant written into the instruction, showed it works the same as the qualifier. In my first probes the constant was passed as a kernel argument instead, and there it changed nothing. I can't explain that difference.

The core assumption I didn't confirm: that ~126 MB of output can be left in L2 to be written back after the timer stops. The evict_last gain is only 3–4 µs, about 25–30 MB of writes, at every size from 1024 to 8192 tokens. One CUPTI timing in the block-order probe failed and was not rerun, so block order is only measured up to 256 tokens.

